First real invocation of AMD's `aiecompiler` (see RESEARCH.md's "Custom C++ XRT /
hand-written AIE kernels") against a trivial ADF graph: a single passthrough
kernel over int8, source at tools/aie_probe/{kernels.h,kernels.cc,graph.h,graph.cc}.
`--target=x86sim` compiles a kernel graph to run as native x86 threads -- it
validates ADF graph structure and kernel logic only, it does not touch, target,
or say anything about the AIE array itself (no tile dtype/timing/placement
information comes from this target). This run's actual result: the compiler
starts, finds and includes the ADF headers, and gets as far as needing a
--platform or --part before it can proceed -- confirming aiecompiler is
functional on this install (contrary to the working assumption two commits ago
that it might not even start) and that specifying no device model at all is not
enough to compile even for a software-only target.

